This repository is publicly accessible, but you have to accept the conditions to access its files and content . Log in or Sign Up to review the conditions and access this model content. Accented English text-to-speech built for the phone, steered by a plain-language voice description. Hurston-1 introduces NB-TTS (Narrowband TTS) : text-to-speech made for the phone line, where most customer service still happens, even when the other side is an AI agent. It learned in part from real phone calls between our contributors and AI voice agents, and its phone mode delivers what a caller actually hears: 8 kHz G.711 audio, ready for Twilio and other telephony systems. Full 24 kHz audio is still the default. Hurston-1 is a fine-tune of Maya1 (Apache-2.0) built by Destined AI to speak English accents that most TTS systems handle poorly: Southern American, Nigerian, West African, East African, Southern African, alongside broader American English. It is the first open model in Destined AI's ClarityVoice family. pip install torch transformers snac soundfile scipy speechbrain # speechbrain is only needed for --check python hurston.py --model . --check \ --accent "Nigerian English accent" --gender Female \ --text "Good afternoon. Please take one tablet twice a day after meals." --out hello.wav # the same line as a caller hears it: 8 kHz G.711 mu-law WAV python hurston.py --model . --check --phone \ --accent "Southern American accent" --gender Male \ --text "Thanks for calling, how can I help you today?" --out call.wav from hurston import Hurston, describe tts = Hurston( '.' , check= True ) # check=True retries takes that Whisper cannot follow or where the voice changes wav = tts( "Well hey there, thanks for calling." , describe( 'Male' , 'adult' , 'Southern American accent' )) The South got something to say In 1995 André 3000 stood on stage at the Source Awards, in front of a crowd that booed OutKast, and said "the South got something to say." We think that line still fits. Hurston-1 was built in Atlanta by a small, independent team. Since 2021 we've been recording consented, paid contributors from the American South and from across Africa. These are voices that most AI systems still flatten into generic American English. Nobody handed us a funding round to do this, so we built it ourselves. These voices belong in AI, and now they have an open model. We named it for Zora Neale Hurston . She grew up in Eatonville, Florida, trained as an anthropologist, and traveled the South recording how Black Southerners actually talked: their stories, songs and speech, at a time when few institutions thought those voices were worth keeping. Our work is a small echo of hers. The name is a tribute; it does not imply any endorsement by her estate. A few lines the way people actually talk around Atlanta. All synthetic; every voice is generated from a description. More samples for every accent are in demo/ (descriptions and text in demo/demos.csv ). The model was trained on descriptions of the form Gender, age, accent, style, pace : Pace labels are absolute speaking rates learned from the natural voices in training data: roughly slow is under 2 words per second, measured 2 to 2.6, natural 2.6 to 3.4, fast over 3.4. The model follows them approximately, not exactly. It still highly matches the behavior of the underlying dataset. Each call samples a new voice that fits the description. Note: Hurston-1 does not clone voices and was not trained to reproduce any real person. Most voice AI is demoed in studio quality and then deployed over a phone line. Hurston-1 is built for where it will actually be heard: from hurston import Hurston, describe, ulaw_encode tts = Hurston( '.' , check= True ) call = tts( "Hi, I'm calling about my appointment on Tuesday." , describe( 'Female' , 'older' , 'Nigerian English accent' ), phone= True ) payload = ulaw_encode(call) # 8 kHz mu-law bytes; base64 it for a Twilio media stream What people build with it Some places to start. At Destined AI we use accented TTS like this to build broad, high-quality test data, and that is still the use we would point to first. Not a fit yet: one consistent brand voice across sessions (each call is a new voice; see Limitations), cloning a specific person, or anything safety-critical without a human listening first. Two questions: does it sound like the accent, and does it say the words? Accent strength vs base Maya1. Base Maya1 documents five accents (American, Indian, Middle Eastern, Asian American, British). For a fair comparison we prompted it in its own documented description style, naming each accent, and compared it with Hurston-1 on the same 10 sentences and 18 voices (180 clips per model). An accent classifier (African vs American English, trained on real speakers) scored each voice group: The classifier cannot tell Southern American from general American English; that accent was checked by listening. Intelligibility. Whisper large-v3-turbo word error rate (WER) against the input text, numbers normalized (lower is better). Everyday sentences (the same 180 clips as the accent test above, natural pacing , single take): Harder sentences (30 unseen sentences × 17 voices, 510 clips): The harder set covers reading, health, customer service, names and numbers, expressive and long sentences. Base Maya1 is very clear because it mostly stays in American English; Hurston-1 keeps nearly the same clarity while actually doing the accent. Names and numbers are the hardest category (WER 0.17 with --check ). Part of that is the recognizer misspelling African names such as Adaeze Okafor rather than the model mispronouncing them. Speaking rate. Median 2.84 words per second with natural pacing , against 3.76 for base Maya1. Hurston-1 speaks a little more deliberately; use fast pacing for quicker delivery. Blind listening. Testers heard the same sentence from base Maya1 and Hurston-1 in random order, without knowing which was which, and chose the one that sounded more like a real person with the requested accent. Hurston-1 was chosen 78% of the time and base Maya1 6% ; the rest were rated a tie or neither (54 comparisons). Base Maya1 was never chosen for an African or Southern American voice; its few wins were on plain American English. LoRA (rank 64) fine-tune of Maya1, 2,000 steps, about 190 hours of speech: Accent labels come from each contributor's self-reported background, checked against an audio accent classifier and human listening; speakers whose audio and labels disagreed were relabeled or removed. Pace labels come from each clip's measured speaking rate. All contributors were adults. The full Destined AI corpus (1,800+ hours, recorded since 2021) is not included in this release; contact us about licensing it. Hurston-1 is the first step. What we are building toward: Follow Destined AI on Hugging Face for the next release. Do not use Hurston-1 to impersonate real people, deceive listeners, or commit fraud. Tell listeners when speech is synthetic. Audio in demo/ is synthetic. @misc{hurston2026, title = {Hurston-1: Accented English Narrowband Text-to-Speech (NB-TTS) for the Phone}, author = {{Destined AI}}, year = {2026}, url = {https://destined.ai} } Downloads last month 3 ","eos_token":"<|eot_id|>","pad_token":"<custom_token_7>"},"chat_template_jinja":"{{- bos_token }}
